            <ArticleTitle>Fractured diaphyseal tibiofibular synostosis in an adolescent soccer player.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>84-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.pmrj.2014.08.947</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1934-1482(14)01357-4</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Diaphyseal tibiofibular synostosis is a rare cause of symptomatic shin pain with exertion. In this case, a 14-year-old male soccer player presented with atraumatic right shin pain made worse with running. Computed tomography revealed heterotopic ossification, or synostosis, of the tibial-fibular syndesmosis. The patient's symptoms improved with rest, without the need for operative intervention.</AbstractText>
